= Smart contract =

== Overview ==
'''Smart contract''' is program code and persistent state deployed to a distributed ledger or blockchain and executed according to that system's transaction and consensus rules.

Smart contracts can implement asset transfers, exchanges, lending, governance rules, registries, games, and other state transitions. Their outputs are validated by the network rather than by a single application server.

The term 'contract' is historical and can be misleading: a smart contract is not necessarily a legally enforceable contract, and code execution does not resolve all questions of legal interpretation or external facts.

Smart contracts have limited direct knowledge of the external world. Systems that depend on off-chain facts often rely on oracles, attestations, or trusted/cryptographically verifiable data sources.

== Boundaries ==
* smart contract ≠ legal contract by definition
* smart contract ≠ automatically autonomous or secure
* on-chain execution ≠ knowledge of external facts

